{-
   Signatures that do not say a consistent thing about ownership.

   `resolveOccurrence` - the one function that reads a parameter, a result and a record field, so
   that the three cannot drift. Everything here is refused before any body is looked at, which is
   what makes it a third file: a declaration this pass rejects has no signature for a body to be
   resolved against.

   Analysis-Borrows-Revised.md §2.2 is the source of the pair at the centre of it. A signature
   states the relationship on **both** ends, so a result that names an origin no parameter published
   is the error rather than an instruction to go and find one - promoting a parameter to satisfy a
   result would change what the caller may do with an argument it never marked.
-}

data Guard {id: I32}

data Pair {left: Guard, right: Guard}

-- Two origins on one occurrence. Peeling the sigils finds `a` and then `b`, and there is no answer
-- to which one the loan is in.
fn twoOrigins(x: a'b'Guard) -> I32 = x.id

-- `->` hands the value over and `'` says somebody else keeps it. The parameter asks for both.
fn sunkAndBorrowed(->x: 'Guard) -> I32 = x.id

-- A result naming an origin nothing published.
fn namedNobodyPublished(p: Pair) -> a'Guard = p.left

-- The same with the anonymous origin, which is a slot like any other and equally has to be
-- introduced by a parameter first.
fn anonymousNobodyPublished(p: Pair) -> 'Guard = p.left

-- A result that borrows and does not say from where. There is nothing at a use site that could work
-- out whose storage a bare `&Guard` result refers to.
fn borrowsFromNowhere(&p: Pair) -> &Guard = p.left

-- And a record field, which is the same question asked of the other schema scope.
data Bad {held: &Guard, at: I32}

-- The older spelling of `'`, in the name area rather than on the type. Refused rather than
-- translated: one spelling per concept is what keeps the two from having to be told apart later.
fn oldSpelling(return x: Guard) -> I32 = x.id

fn main() -> I32 = 0
